The callback loses its `this` binding.

## Cause

`setInterval` calls its callback as a plain function, not as a method. In a regular `function () {}`, `this` depends on how the function gets called, not on where you defined it. Inside a class body, code runs in strict mode, so `this` inside the callback is `undefined` instead of falling back to the global object.

`this.seconds` then reads a property off `undefined`. That throws, except when the callback runs, JavaScript first evaluates `this.seconds += 1` as `this.seconds = this.seconds + 1`. Since `this` is `undefined`, `this.seconds` itself throws a `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`.

